{"cuisine/mesoamerican-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/mesoamerican-cuisine","n":"Mesoamerican cuisine","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Mexican cuisine","cuisine/mexican-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Mexico","intro":[["Mesoamerican cuisine (covering Belize, El Salvador, Guatemala, Honduras, Nicaragua, northern Costa Rica and Mexico) has four main staples: maize (many varieties based on what climate it is grown in), beans, squash and chili. Other plant-based foods used include amaranth, avocado, cassava, cherimoya, chia, chocolate, guava, nanche, pineapple, sapodilla, sweet potatoes, yucca and zapote."],["Historically, various methods and techniques were employed to store, prepare and preserve the foods, most of which remain in use today. Hernán Cortés introduced rice and wheat to Mesoamerica, prior to which time milpa (known as the cornfield) was one of the main sources of sustenance."],["Some traditional foods featured in the cuisine include atole (a drink made using masa) and chocolate atole (with the addition of chocolate), also known as champurrado. Two classic maize preparations are boiling maize in water and lime, mixing with chili peppers and eating as gruel, and dough preparation for flat cakes, tamales and tortillas. Edible foam is another popular food item, sometimes even regarded as sacred."],["While squashes were cooked for food, dried gourds were repurposed for storage or used during battles with embers and chilies, wrapped in leaves and used as chemical warfare."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"mesoamerican-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mesoamerican_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Mexican cuisine","cuisine/mexican-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Mexican cuisine","cuisine/mexican-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Within Mesoamerican cooking","count":2,"links":[["Ancient Maya cuisine","cuisine/ancient-maya-cuisine"],["Aztec cuisine","cuisine/aztec-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas"},"cuisine/tex-mex-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/tex-mex-cuisine","n":"Tex-Mex cuisine","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Mexican-American cuisine","cuisine/mexican-american-cuisine"],["Texan cuisine","cuisine/texan-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in United States of America","intro":[["Tex-Mex cuisine (derived from the words Texas and Mexico), or New Tejano cuisine, or simply Tejano cuisine is a regional American cuisine that originates from the culinary creations of Tejano people inspired in Mexican culinary traditions. It has spread from border states such as Texas and others in the Southwestern United States to the rest of the country. It is a subtype of Southwestern cuisine found in the American Southwest."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"tex-mex-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tex-Mex_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Homesick Texan","ref":"site/homesicktexan-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"homesicktexan.com","note":"Lisa Fain's long-running blog of Texan and Tex-Mex home cooking.","url":"https://www.homesicktexan.com/homesick-texan-cookbook-introduction/comment-page-20/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"The Homesick Texan Cookbook","ref":"cookbook/the-homesick-texan-cookbook","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Lisa Fain, 2011","note":"Texas home cooking with many Tex-Mex staples, from the Homesick Texan blog.","url":"https://www.hachettebookgroup.com/titles/lisa-fain/the-homesick-texan-cookbook/9781401324261","suggested":true,"isbn":"9781401324261"}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":14,"links":[["Baja Fresh","dish/baja-fresh"],["Chapultepec Lupita","dish/chapultepec-lupita"],["Chile con queso","dish/chile-con-queso"],["Chili con carne","dish/chili-con-carne"],["Chipotle Mexican Grill","dish/chipotle-mexican-grill"],["Chuy's","dish/chuy-s"],["Esparza's","dish/esparza-s"],["Guacamole","dish/guacamole"],["La Bamba Mexican Restaurant","dish/la-bamba-mexican-restaurant"],["Pancho Villa (restaurant)","dish/pancho-villa-restaurant"],["Salsarita's Fresh Mexican Grill","dish/salsarita-s-fresh-mexican-grill"],["The Goose (restaurant)","dish/the-goose-restaurant"],["Tia's Tex-Mex","dish/tia-s-tex-mex"],["Tortilla Coast","dish/tortilla-coast"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Cheese","ingredient/cheese"],["Cream","ingredient/cream"],["Cream cheese","ingredient/cream-cheese"],["Monterey Jack","ingredient/monterey-jack"],["Processed cheese","ingredient/processed-cheese"],["Velveeta","ingredient/velveeta"],["Ground meat","ingredient/ground-meat"],["Tomato","ingredient/tomato"],["Avocado","ingredient/avocado"],["Edible salt","ingredient/edible-salt"],["Jalapeño","ingredient/jalapeno"],["Lime (fruit)","ingredient/lime-fruit"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Mexican-American cuisine","cuisine/mexican-american-cuisine"],["Texan cuisine","cuisine/texan-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/3/3a/NCI_Visuals_Food_Taco.jpg/500px-NCI_Visuals_Food_Taco.jpg","w":500,"h":370,"by":"Renee Comet (photographer)","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NCI_Visuals_Food_Taco.jpg"}},"ingredient/oyster":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/oyster","n":"Oyster","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 6 dishes across 5 cuisines","intro":[["Oyster is the common name for a number of different families of salt-water bivalve molluscs that live in marine or brackish habitats. In some species, the valves are highly calcified, and many are somewhat irregular in shape. Many (but not all) oysters are in the superfamily Ostreoidea."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-oyster"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oyster"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":5,"groups":[["Cali-Mex cuisine","cuisine/cuisine-of-california-latino-and-hispanic-influence",[["Hangtown fry","dish/hangtown-fry"],["Oysters Kirkpatrick","dish/oysters-kirkpatrick"]]],["Cuisine of California","cuisine/cuisine-of-california",[["Hangtown fry","dish/hangtown-fry"],["Oysters Kirkpatrick","dish/oysters-kirkpatrick"]]],["Louisiana Creole cuisine","cuisine/louisiana-creole-cuisine",[["Oysters Bienville","dish/oysters-bienville"],["Oysters Rockefeller","dish/oysters-rockefeller"]]],["English cuisine","cuisine/english-cuisine",[["Angels on horseback","dish/angels-on-horseback"]]],["Japanese cuisine","cuisine/japanese-cuisine",[["Kaki furai","dish/kaki-furai"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Bacon","ingredient/bacon"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Panko","ingredient/panko"],["Mushroom","ingredient/mushroom"],["Parmesan","ingredient/parmesan"],["Shrimp","ingredient/shrimp"],["Cheese","ingredient/cheese"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Scallion","ingredient/scallion"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/b/b0/Crassostrea_gigas_p1040848.jpg/500px-Crassostrea_gigas_p1040848.jpg","w":500,"h":348,"by":"David.Monniaux","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Crassostrea_gigas_p1040848.jpg"}},"dish/boerenjongens":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/boerenjongens","n":"Boerenjongens","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Dutch cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Boerenjongens is a Dutch dish of raisins soaked in brandy. The name means \"farmer boys\" in Dutch. This recipe is traditionally consumed at holidays or birthdays, and is often a topping option at pancake restaurants. In the city of Groningen and other cities in the countryside like Enschede there is often boerenjongens-flavoured ice cream for sale."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-boerenjongens"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boerenjongens"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Dutch cuisine","cuisine/dutch-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":2,"links":[["Brandy","ingredient/brandy"],["Raisin","ingredient/raisin"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"By Andrea Janssen","ref":"site/byandreajanssen-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"byandreajanssen.com","note":"Dutch food blogger writing in English about Dutch and everyday home recipes.","url":"https://www.byandreajanssen.com/about/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Dutch cuisine","links":[["De Librije","dish/de-librije"],["Lekkerbekje","dish/lekkerbekje"],["Oliebol","dish/oliebol"],["Pepernoot","dish/pepernoot"],["Speculaas","dish/speculaas"],["Stamppot","dish/stamppot"],["Uitsmijter","dish/uitsmijter"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/2/2a/Boerenjongens.jpg/500px-Boerenjongens.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"frank wouters from antwerpen, belgium , België , Belgique","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boerenjongens.jpg"}},"dish/chalbori-ppang":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/chalbori-ppang","n":"Chalbori-ppang","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from South Korean cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Chalbori-ppang is a South Korean confection, consisting of two small pancakes made with glutinous barley flour wrapped around a filling of red bean paste. The round, flat, mildly sweet confection has a texture similar to that of a glutinous sponge cake."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-chalbori-ppang"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chalbori-ppang"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["South Korean cuisine","cuisine/south-korean-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":4,"links":[["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Glutinous barley","ingredient/glutinous-barley"],["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Red bean paste","ingredient/red-bean-paste"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Korean Bapsang","ref":"site/koreanbapsang-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"koreanbapsang.com","note":"Home-style Korean recipes from Hyosun, a Korean-American mom.","url":"https://www.koreanbapsang.com/category/recipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from South Korean cuisine","links":[["Chimaek","dish/chimaek"],["Croffle","dish/croffle"],["Dak-galbi","dish/dak-galbi"],["Gilgeori toast","dish/gilgeori-toast"],["Hodu-gwaja","dish/hodu-gwaja"],["Hwangnam-ppang","dish/hwangnam-ppang"],["Jinju bibimbap","dish/jinju-bibimbap"],["Jjolmyeon","dish/jjolmyeon"],["Kkulppang","dish/kkulppang"],["Milmyeon","dish/milmyeon"],["Rabokki","dish/rabokki"],["So-tteok-so-tteok","dish/so-tteok-so-tteok"],["Tornado potato","dish/tornado-potato"],["Ttongppang","dish/ttongppang"],["Yangnyeom chicken","dish/yangnyeom-chicken"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/2/29/Chalbori-ppang_2.jpg/500px-Chalbori-ppang_2.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"퓨라이트","lic":"CC BY 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chalbori-ppang_2.jpg"}},"dish/dadiah":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/dadiah","n":"Dadiah","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Indonesian cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Dadiah (Minangkabau) or dadih (Indonesian and Malaysian Malay) is a traditional fermented milk popular among people of West Sumatra, Indonesia. It is made by pouring fresh, raw, unheated, buffalo milk into a bamboo tube capped with a banana leaf and allowing it to ferment spontaneously at room temperature for two days."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-dadiah"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dadiah"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":1,"links":[["Milk","ingredient/milk"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Daily Cooking Quest","ref":"site/dailycookingquest-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"dailycookingquest.com","note":"Indonesian home cook Anita's blog with a large archive of Indonesian recipes.","url":"https://dailycookingquest.com/cuisines/indonesian.html","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Indonesian cuisine","links":[["Arem-arem","dish/arem-arem"],["Arsik","dish/arsik"],["Asam pedas","dish/asam-pedas"],["Ayam goreng","dish/ayam-goreng"],["Ayam goreng kalasan","dish/ayam-goreng-kalasan"],["Ayam panggang","dish/ayam-panggang"],["Ayam Taliwang","dish/ayam-taliwang"],["Bajigur","dish/bajigur"],["Bakakak hayam","dish/bakakak-hayam"],["Bakpia","dish/bakpia"],["Bakwan","dish/bakwan"],["Bekasang","dish/bekasang"],["Betutu","dish/betutu"],["Bika ambon","dish/bika-ambon"],["Binte biluhuta","dish/binte-biluhuta"],["Bir pletok","dish/bir-pletok"],["Bolu kukus","dish/bolu-kukus"],["Botok","dish/botok"],["Brem","dish/brem"],["Bubur kacang hijau","dish/bubur-kacang-hijau"],["Bubur ketan hitam","dish/bubur-ketan-hitam"],["Bubur pedas","dish/bubur-pedas"],["Buntil","dish/buntil"],["Burgo (food)","dish/burgo-food"],["Cendol","dish/cendol"],["Cenil","dish/cenil"],["Cilok","dish/cilok"],["Cimpa","dish/cimpa"],["Corn fritter","dish/corn-fritter"],["Coto makassar","dish/coto-makassar"],["Dadar gulung","dish/dadar-gulung"],["Dengke mas naniura","dish/dengke-mas-naniura"],["Empal gentong","dish/empal-gentong"],["Emping","dish/emping"],["Es buah","dish/es-buah"],["Es doger","dish/es-doger"],["Es goyobod","dish/es-goyobod"],["Gado-gado","dish/gado-gado"],["Geplak","dish/geplak"],["Gepuk","dish/gepuk"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/8/8a/Dadiah.jpg/500px-Dadiah.jpg","w":500,"h":500,"by":"Adhmi","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dadiah.jpg"}},"ingredient/sesame-paste":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/sesame-paste","n":"Sesame paste","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 2 cuisines","intro":[["Tahini is a Middle Eastern condiment (a seed butter) made from ground sesame seeds. The most common variety comes from hulled seeds, but unhulled ones can also be used; the latter variety is slightly bitter, but more nutritious. The seeds are more commonly roasted than raw. Tahini can be served by itself (as a dip), made into a salad dressing, or used as a major ingredient in hummus, baba ghanoush, or halva."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-sesame-paste"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sesame_paste"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine",[["Hot dry noodles","dish/hot-dry-noodles"]]],["Hubei cuisine","cuisine/hubei-cuisine",[["Hot dry noodles","dish/hot-dry-noodles"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Alkaline noodles","ingredient/alkaline-noodles"],["Chili oil","ingredient/chili-oil"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Pickled carrots","ingredient/pickled-carrots"],["Soy sauce","ingredient/soy-sauce"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/3/39/Tahina.JPG/500px-Tahina.JPG","w":500,"h":376,"by":"Gilabrand","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tahina.JPG"}},"dish/klein-paardenburg":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/klein-paardenburg","n":"Klein Paardenburg","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 2 cuisines","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Bistro Klein Paardenburg was a restaurant in Ouderkerk aan de Amstel, in the Netherlands. It was a fine dining restaurant that was awarded one Michelin star in 1976 and retained that rating until 1979. In 1985, it was again awarded a Michelin star and retained that rating until 1990. The restaurant was unexpectedly closed down on 31 January 2011, for no obvious reason."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-klein-paardenburg"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Klein_Paardenburg"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine"],["New Caledonian cuisine","cuisine/new-caledonian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Pardon Your French","ref":"site/pardonyourfrench-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"pardonyourfrench.com","note":"Audrey Le Goff, a French cook, shares classic and regional French home recipes.","url":"https://pardonyourfrench.com","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"La Cuisine de Géraldine","ref":"site/lacuisinedegeraldine-fr","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"lacuisinedegeraldine.fr","note":"French home cooking blog by Géraldine Leverd, also author of The New French Kitchen.","url":"https://lacuisinedegeraldine.fr/en/about-us","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"New Caledonia Tourism: Kanak cooking","ref":"site/newcaledonia-travel","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"newcaledonia.travel","note":"Official tourism board overview of Kanak food, including bougna cooked in an earth oven.","url":"https://www.newcaledonia.travel/nz/gastronomy-wine/gastronomy/kanak-cooking","suggested":true,"kind":"site"}]},{"h":"More from French cuisine","links":[["'t Fornuis","dish/t-fornuis"],["'t Misverstant","dish/t-misverstant"],["'t Vlasbloemeken","dish/t-vlasbloemeken"],["&moshik","dish/moshik"],["Aan de Poel","dish/aan-de-poel"],["Adrian (restaurant)","dish/adrian-restaurant"],["Adventureland Bazaar","dish/adventureland-bazaar"],["Alain Ducasse at The Dorchester","dish/alain-ducasse-at-the-dorchester"],["Albufera sauce","dish/albufera-sauce"],["Alex (restaurant)","dish/alex-restaurant"],["Andouillette","dish/andouillette"],["Arpège (restaurant)","dish/arpege-restaurant"],["Aspic","dish/aspic"],["Atelier Crenn","dish/atelier-crenn"],["Au Coin des Bons Enfants","dish/au-coin-des-bons-enfants"],["Au Crocodile","dish/au-crocodile"],[